From ea23fc6153d0e0673f5ce512a366e39357be68cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 06:40:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let a button do a second thing when it is held

A toolbar has five places and a browser has more than five things worth
reaching for. Holding a button is a gesture nobody is using: it currently does
nothing on most of them, and Bare already had to go out of its way to make sure
it did nothing rather than firing the tap late.

The button stays one button. It is registered once, under the action it
performs when tapped, and the bar's idea of which action lives where does not
change; what changes is that the model it already publishes is given something
to run on a long press. That is why the hold is allowed to repeat what other
buttons do. Two positions may both offer Share on a hold, and a position may
offer on a hold what it already does on a tap, because a hold is only ever
asked of the one button being held. The tap keeps its old rule, where choosing
a button that is already elsewhere swaps the two, since only the tap has to
answer the question of where a button is.

What runs is looked up when the finger lifts rather than when the preference
was written. A button that offers Translate on a hold has to mean the Translate
that applies to the page in front of the user, and on a page with nothing to
translate there is nothing to do rather than something stale to do. The same
model the action already publishes is used, so holding a button to bookmark
bookmarks the page exactly as pressing a bookmark button would, down to the
same listener.

Choosing nothing means the button keeps whatever it already did when held. For
most of them that is Bare swallowing the gesture so it does not arrive late as
a tap; for the home button it is the homepage menu, which is not a callback on
the model but a delegate the generic binder cannot see. So the home button's
own binder decides that case: whenever something re-decides what a long press
does, it puts the menu back unless a second action has been chosen to run
instead. The menu is a property of the home button rather than of the order its
properties happened to arrive in, and choosing nothing gives it back at once
rather than leaving the button silent until the bar is next rebuilt.

One touch does one thing. Holding still long enough runs the second action and
the bar then leaves the rest of that touch alone, so sliding away afterwards
does not also change the brightness; moving sideways first cancels the button
before its hold ever fires, which the swipe already relied on. Neither of those
is new machinery, only the two of them agreeing on which one has the gesture.

In settings, pressing a position now asks about that position rather than
straight about the toolbar, because a button that does two things needs both of
them visible: a hold that is never shown anywhere is a hold nobody knows they
have.
